Cervical Dystonia
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Involuntary patterned head deviation, Sensory trick (geste antagoniste) relief, Hypertrophied cervical musculature

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-007
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, unrelenting neck spasm causing extreme pain and inability to swallow safely.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported severe, locked rotational head deviation accompanied by severe, localized aching pain and subjective dysphagia. Immediate assessment was strictly necessary to rule out acute structural cervical spine pathology and provide urgent muscular relief before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My neck twisted to the right three hours ago and locked. The pain is agonizing and I choke when I try to drink water."
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a sudden, dramatic exacerbation of involuntary, sustained contractions of the neck musculature leading to an abnormal head posture. The current attack began earlier this evening following severe psychological stress at work. The patient exhibits severe rotational head deviation (torticollis) to the right. There is severe, localized aching pain directly originating from the constantly contracting cervical musculature. The patient reports a transient, complete relief of the spasm by utilizing a sensory trick (geste antagoniste), specifically applying a light touch to the right cheek, but the relief vanishes the moment the hand is removed. There are distinctly jerky, tremulous head movements accompanying the sustained directional pull.
Functional Status: Temporarily incapacitated by severe pain and fixed visual field.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to drive or safely perform self-care tasks.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Sitting rigidly, head forcefully rotated and tilted to the right, visibly distressed and exhausted.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal examination reveals heavily thickened, hypertrophied, and palpably tense left sternocleidomastoid and right posterior cervical musculature. The patient exhibits an involuntary patterned head deviation to the right. The application of a light finger touch to the right jaw (sensory trick) temporarily neutralizes the pull. Asymmetrical elevation of the right shoulder is accompanying the primary head deviation. Neurological examination confirms the complete absence of objective sensory deficits, generalized muscle weakness, or long tract neurological signs.
Environmental Observations: Safe, clear living environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No current medications for this condition. Nil allergies.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cervical Dystonia (Spasmodic Torticollis) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Cervical Radiculopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of radiating neuropathic pain, numbness, or reflex changes in the upper extremities.

Posterior Fossa Tumor - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of ataxia, cranial nerve palsies, or increased intracranial pressure signs.

Retropharyngeal Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile, absence of severe throat erythema, and normal vital signs aside from pain-related hypertension.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, hyper-kinetic dystonic crisis causing severe biomechanical impairment and 9/10 localized pain. The fixed cervical rotation is impeding normal swallowing, raising the immediate risk of aspiration. Delaying clinical assessment until the morning would subject the patient to continuous, excruciating pain, profound sleep deprivation, and potential dehydration. Urgent after-hours assessment allowed for the definitive clinical exclusion of dangerous secondary causes of torticollis (e.g., epidural abscess, acute disc prolapse) and the immediate administration of targeted, high-dose anticholinergic/muscle relaxant therapy to acutely break the dystonic cycle.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Diazepam 5mg orally STAT, followed by 5mg twice daily for 3 days. Prescribed Benztropine 1mg orally STAT to target the dystonic mechanism.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By actively utilizing the sensory trick (touching the cheek) whenever attempting to swallow, the patient instantly neutralizes the neck spasm, ensuring safe and pain-free hydration.
Step 2: By lying supine with a low-profile pillow, the patient completely removes gravity from the head, directly unloading the cervical muscles and facilitating rest.
Step 3: By identifying and actively stepping away from acute stressful situations, the patient removes the primary neurogenic trigger, preventing the rapid escalation of future dystonic spasms.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days; referral to neurology for Botulinum toxin therapy consideration.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Attend the Emergency Department immediately if you experience complete inability to swallow saliva, severe difficulty breathing, or sudden numbness/weakness in your arms or legs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a clinical diagnosis; specialized neurological assessment is recommended for long-term management.
Clinical Course: The acute severe spasm should soften within 60 to 90 minutes of medication administration. The underlying condition is chronic and may require targeted injections for definitive control.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for urgent medical review if the pain completely fails to respond to the prescribed medications after 4 hours, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The brain is sending continuous, faulty signals to your neck muscles, locking them in place. The prescribed medications work together to block these specific chemical signals and force the muscles to relax.
Actionable Steps: Take the medications immediately. Lie flat to rest your neck. Use your finger on your cheek when you need to drink.

